WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:04.046
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:04.046 --> 00:00:05.682
It's Evan, my one word is believe

00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:07.825
and I believe in people
more than they believe

00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:10.609
in themselves and my sincere hope is that

00:00:10.609 --> 00:00:13.664
if you see in yourself what I see in you,

00:00:13.664 --> 00:00:15.851
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:15.851 --> 00:00:17.161
And so to help you on your journey,

00:00:17.161 --> 00:00:19.762
we're continuing the top five book series

00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:21.814
where we basically look at five books

00:00:21.814 --> 00:00:24.597
that are trending on Amazon
in a certain category

00:00:24.597 --> 00:00:27.585
and thinking might be of
interest and value to you guys,

00:00:27.585 --> 00:00:29.529
entrepreneurs, trying
to build your business.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.449
And so today we're looking
at the top five books

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:34.094
for small business owners.

00:00:34.094 --> 00:00:35.733
Coming in at number five is

00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:39.816
The Hard Thing About Hard
Things by Ben Horowitz.

00:00:42.815 --> 00:00:44.663
- Ben Horowitz, you were
recently referred to

00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:46.716
as the CEO Whisperer.

00:00:46.716 --> 00:00:49.481
Is your new book The Hard
Thing About Hard Things

00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:52.953
a way to stop whispering and
get the word out more widely?

00:00:52.953 --> 00:00:56.569
- Yeah, I'm going to step my
game up to the CEO Shouter.

00:00:56.569 --> 00:00:59.221
But actually, the reason I wrote the book

00:00:59.221 --> 00:01:02.163
was when I was CEO, I
would have these times

00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:04.726
where I'd be up at two
o'clock in the morning

00:01:04.726 --> 00:01:08.884
in a cold sweat and trying
to figure out why none

00:01:08.884 --> 00:01:11.526
of the management books
that I read were helping me.

00:01:11.526 --> 00:01:14.563
You know, I was just stuck,
and what I came to realize

00:01:14.563 --> 00:01:16.874
was most of the books that I had read

00:01:16.874 --> 00:01:19.938
were about here are all
the things that you do

00:01:19.938 --> 00:01:21.574
to not screw up your company.

00:01:21.574 --> 00:01:24.784
But once your company is
screwed up, you're on your own.

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.448
And it turns out that the really hard part

00:01:27.448 --> 00:01:29.866
about running a company
is when things go wrong,

00:01:29.866 --> 00:01:33.299
or when you get in a very
emotionally difficult situation.

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:34.681
And there wasn't anything for that,

00:01:34.681 --> 00:01:36.933
so that's kind of what
prompted me to write the book.

00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:41.100
- Coming in at number four is
#GIRLBOSS by Sophia Amoruso.

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:47.200
- Did you ever think that it
would be such a phenomenon?

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.282
- No, I mean, I think
everybody wants their book

00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:51.365
to be a bestseller and
so it wasn't something

00:01:51.365 --> 00:01:53.743
that we, you know, that
we didn't talk about,

00:01:53.743 --> 00:01:55.550
but, you know, I'd never
written a book before,

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.236
I didn't know how that whole world works.

00:01:57.236 --> 00:01:59.326
It was very new to me, and yeah,

00:01:59.326 --> 00:02:02.059
it was just kind of like,
it's an easy book to read

00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:05.771
on a Sunday, it's not super
heavy, but it's entertaining.

00:02:05.771 --> 00:02:08.177
It's not like if you do these five things,

00:02:08.177 --> 00:02:10.107
this will happen to you,
a lot of storytelling

00:02:10.107 --> 00:02:12.122
and anecdotes and fun quotes.

00:02:12.122 --> 00:02:14.439
- What's your favorite story
in here, what's your go-to?

00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:15.893
- Oh, God.

00:02:15.893 --> 00:02:18.039
I mean, I think, you
know, a lot of people ask,

00:02:18.039 --> 00:02:21.158
"When was the moment that was
like a turning point for you?"

00:02:21.158 --> 00:02:23.921
And for me, it was a really crazy time.

00:02:23.921 --> 00:02:27.673
I was on eBay, and went
into a thrift store,

00:02:27.673 --> 00:02:30.088
was just looking around
and found a Chanel jacket

00:02:30.088 --> 00:02:32.536
for eight dollars and you know,

00:02:32.536 --> 00:02:33.951
had no idea how much it was worth,

00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:35.367
but I was like, "Wow,
Chanel eight dollars.

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:36.325
"Cool, I'm going to buy it."

00:02:36.325 --> 00:02:38.149
Put it on eBay for $9.99.

00:02:38.149 --> 00:02:40.354
It could've sold for $9.99, but of course,

00:02:40.354 --> 00:02:42.518
people bid on it, it's an auction,

00:02:42.518 --> 00:02:46.368
and after a week, it went
for, like, over $1,000,

00:02:46.368 --> 00:02:47.790
and that was just, like, yeah,

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.385
just such a cool vote of confidence

00:02:49.385 --> 00:02:50.783
that something was working.

00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:54.950
- Coming it an number three
is Shoe Dog by Phil Knight.

00:02:57.356 --> 00:03:00.134
- Other people have
written books about Nike.

00:03:00.134 --> 00:03:01.802
This is your first time.

00:03:01.802 --> 00:03:02.742
Take us back.

00:03:02.742 --> 00:03:04.875
You're out of school, your dad said,

00:03:04.875 --> 00:03:06.805
"You've got this crazy idea,"
that's what you kept saying.

00:03:06.805 --> 00:03:08.447
"This crazy idea."

00:03:08.447 --> 00:03:09.766
How did that happen?

00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:12.265
- Well, basically, it was
generated from two places.

00:03:12.265 --> 00:03:13.758
Basically, the running track at Oregon

00:03:13.758 --> 00:03:16.135
where Bowerman was always
fiddling with shoes.

00:03:16.135 --> 00:03:17.866
And then, at Stanford Business School,

00:03:17.866 --> 00:03:20.737
I took an entrepreneurship
class where I wrote about,

00:03:20.737 --> 00:03:24.496
you know, the track shoe industry
and how to break into it.

00:03:24.496 --> 00:03:26.611
So the two ideas kept growing,

00:03:26.611 --> 00:03:28.651
and then finally I went
to my father and said,

00:03:28.651 --> 00:03:30.292
"I need to pursue this a bit."

00:03:30.292 --> 00:03:33.890
- Was there ever a time that
you thought, "This isn't..."

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.366
I mean, when you read the book, I mean,

00:03:35.366 --> 00:03:36.910
even though I know the outcome,

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.003
there are times I'm reading it and saying,

00:03:38.003 --> 00:03:38.836
"He's not going to make it!"

00:03:38.836 --> 00:03:39.669
"He's not going to make it."

00:03:39.669 --> 00:03:42.173
- Oh, absolutely, we were right
on the edge a couple times

00:03:42.173 --> 00:03:44.031
that I know I say, "Was
there ever a time?"

00:03:44.031 --> 00:03:45.112
Yeah, there were some times.

00:03:45.112 --> 00:03:47.438
The most scary was when we
got kicked out of the bank

00:03:47.438 --> 00:03:49.378
for the second time and they told me

00:03:49.378 --> 00:03:50.841
that they turned me in to the FBI,

00:03:50.841 --> 00:03:53.152
and I thought, "Well, these
are a little tough times."

00:03:53.152 --> 00:03:56.788
That they can't take the house, (laughs)

00:03:56.788 --> 00:03:59.308
but I did say if we failed,
I was going to try it again

00:03:59.308 --> 00:04:00.682
'cause I was enjoying it so much.

00:04:00.682 --> 00:04:02.696
- And what do you say to,
'cause we've had a lot

00:04:02.696 --> 00:04:05.004
of young people that are
starting their businesses

00:04:05.004 --> 00:04:07.135
and there are a lot of people who watch,

00:04:07.135 --> 00:04:09.780
and they have a passion,
they want to have fun.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.064
What do you say to a young entrepreneur,

00:04:11.064 --> 00:04:11.897
or any entrepreneur?

00:04:11.897 --> 00:04:13.329
- Well, I think there's a couple things,

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:15.044
that if you're going
to be an entrepreneur,

00:04:15.044 --> 00:04:16.574
you'd better be prepared for long hours

00:04:16.574 --> 00:04:18.110
and a lot of dark moments.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.792
And I guess that's one thing
that's shown in the book.

00:04:20.792 --> 00:04:23.077
And I think, so you really
have to have a passion

00:04:23.077 --> 00:04:25.932
about it and you have to
have a reason to succeed.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:28.695
It isn't to be just something
that you want to be,

00:04:28.695 --> 00:04:30.575
that you have to have
a niche and a passion.

00:04:30.575 --> 00:04:31.631
You need those two things.

00:04:31.631 --> 00:04:35.798
- Coming in at number two is
Elon Musk by Ashlee Vance.

00:04:38.764 --> 00:04:43.495
- So, in 2012, I did a Business
Week cover story on Elon,

00:04:43.495 --> 00:04:46.450
and you know, we got along pretty well.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.193
I got to spend a bunch of time with him,

00:04:49.193 --> 00:04:50.809
going through the SpaceX factory,

00:04:50.809 --> 00:04:54.328
going to movie premieres
and all this stuff.

00:04:54.328 --> 00:04:57.177
And, you know, the companies
were very interesting

00:04:57.177 --> 00:04:59.008
and Elon was much more interesting

00:04:59.008 --> 00:05:02.822
than I'd given him credit
for before the story.

00:05:02.822 --> 00:05:04.575
He, you know, I'd sort of
pegged him as just, like,

00:05:04.575 --> 00:05:07.440
a one-note techno utopian kind of guy,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.918
and you know, he turned out
to be this great interview.

00:05:09.918 --> 00:05:12.732
He didn't have PR handlers around him,

00:05:12.732 --> 00:05:15.465
so you had access to
him and he would answer

00:05:15.465 --> 00:05:18.980
just about anything you asked him.

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.813
And so I decided then,

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:23.248
"This is a guy I really
want to write about,"

00:05:23.248 --> 00:05:25.137
and I'd been looking for a book to do,

00:05:25.137 --> 00:05:28.134
and so after I did the story,
a couple months went by

00:05:28.134 --> 00:05:32.337
and then I'd started to feel
Elon out about doing a book.

00:05:32.337 --> 00:05:34.393
And I think I either sent him an email

00:05:34.393 --> 00:05:37.713
or called him on the phone, and I said,

00:05:37.713 --> 00:05:38.546
"This is what I want to do."

00:05:38.546 --> 00:05:40.066
And he said, "Look, a bunch
of people have asked me

00:05:40.066 --> 00:05:43.149
"in the past and I'm not going to do it.

00:05:44.422 --> 00:05:46.020
"I'm not going to help you do the book."

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.541
And then, I went to New York
and I sold the book anyway.

00:05:49.541 --> 00:05:50.633
(laughing)

00:05:50.633 --> 00:05:53.556
I thought I would sort of
force his hand a little bit,

00:05:53.556 --> 00:05:56.337
and then I arranged
another meeting with him,

00:05:56.337 --> 00:05:59.063
and I'll never forget, it
was on a Saturday at Tesla.

00:05:59.063 --> 00:05:59.967
And I came in and I was like,

00:05:59.967 --> 00:06:01.176
"Look, I've sold the book,"

00:06:01.176 --> 00:06:05.026
and he said, "I'm still not
going to cooperate with you."

00:06:05.026 --> 00:06:08.073
And so I spent, like, 18 months

00:06:08.073 --> 00:06:09.414
interviewing hundreds of people.

00:06:09.414 --> 00:06:13.518
I found, like, all these
SpaceX and Tesla employees,

00:06:13.518 --> 00:06:16.424
most of whom had left, all
his Zip2, PayPal people

00:06:16.424 --> 00:06:18.232
that he'd worked with, his ex girlfriends,

00:06:18.232 --> 00:06:20.934
his, like, childhood
friends and all that stuff.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:23.962
And then, you know, a lot
of those people would,

00:06:23.962 --> 00:06:26.003
they would call back to Elon and say,

00:06:26.003 --> 00:06:27.466
"Should I speak to him or not?"

00:06:27.466 --> 00:06:28.980
and he was a pretty good sport about it.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.972
There's nobody that he told not to,

00:06:30.972 --> 00:06:33.044
and so I think that happened enough times

00:06:33.044 --> 00:06:36.233
that after about 18 months of doing it,

00:06:36.233 --> 00:06:40.775
I was at home, it was, like,
6:30 at night on a weekday

00:06:40.775 --> 00:06:43.497
and up on my caller ID,
it was just, "Elon Musk."

00:06:43.497 --> 00:06:44.609
(laughing)

00:06:44.609 --> 00:06:47.450
And I picked up the phone and he said,

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:50.893
"Look, I can basically, this
is going to go one of two ways.

00:06:50.893 --> 00:06:53.870
"I can shut down all
these people that call me

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.343
"and ask whether, you know, I
should let them talk to you,

00:06:57.343 --> 00:06:59.710
"or I could cooperate with the book."

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.855
I was like, "Obviously, you
should choose option number B."

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:07.022
Or option B, and then we set
up a meeting to talk about it.

00:07:09.625 --> 00:07:11.604
He wanted to be able to, he wanted

00:07:11.604 --> 00:07:14.154
to have some control over it, initially.

00:07:14.154 --> 00:07:17.476
He wanted to be able to read
the book before it came out,

00:07:17.476 --> 00:07:19.538
and then he wanted to put
footnotes in the book,

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:22.372
and if you know anything about Elon,

00:07:22.372 --> 00:07:25.328
that would mean the footnotes
would be longer than the book

00:07:25.328 --> 00:07:28.569
and that the book would
probably never be published.

00:07:28.569 --> 00:07:31.261
And so I kind of pushed
back on him on that.

00:07:31.261 --> 00:07:33.516
I had this huge speech
prepared and I got, like,

00:07:33.516 --> 00:07:36.493
five minute into it and he
just said, "Okay," and yeah.

00:07:36.493 --> 00:07:40.496
To his credit, after
that, he gave me access

00:07:40.496 --> 00:07:43.249
to everybody I wanted and then he agreed

00:07:43.249 --> 00:07:45.717
to have a meeting with me once a month,

00:07:45.717 --> 00:07:46.865
he said for as long as it took.

00:07:46.865 --> 00:07:48.399
It ended up being about eight months

00:07:48.399 --> 00:07:51.376
and we would have, like,
three, four, five hour dinners.

00:07:51.376 --> 00:07:54.230
- What is it like to
spend a few hours a month

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:55.947
interviewing Elon Musk?

00:07:55.947 --> 00:07:57.614
- It was really fun.

00:07:58.538 --> 00:07:59.747
At the beginning, it was nerveracking

00:07:59.747 --> 00:08:02.164
'cause I never knew how
many of these interviews

00:08:02.164 --> 00:08:05.088
I would actually get even
though he had agreed to it.

00:08:05.088 --> 00:08:09.101
I always figured, sort
of, that he might do one

00:08:09.101 --> 00:08:12.258
and then he'd be like,
"Okay, that's enough."

00:08:12.258 --> 00:08:14.051
And then I thought he might get sick of it

00:08:14.051 --> 00:08:15.392
after awhile and just cut me off.

00:08:15.392 --> 00:08:19.864
So each one sort of felt
like an accomplishment,

00:08:19.864 --> 00:08:23.235
but then it was frustrating
because the first four,

00:08:23.235 --> 00:08:25.022
I really didn't, I was getting lots

00:08:25.022 --> 00:08:27.633
of useful stuff, but nothing that was,

00:08:27.633 --> 00:08:29.329
I could tell he was
still holding back a bit

00:08:29.329 --> 00:08:32.621
and he was repeating
a lot of stories that,

00:08:32.621 --> 00:08:35.668
if you watch Elon's
speeches and other talks,

00:08:35.668 --> 00:08:38.269
that you would've heard before.

00:08:38.269 --> 00:08:40.209
And so that was frustrating.

00:08:40.209 --> 00:08:42.343
And then, I'm still not
sure exactly what happened,

00:08:42.343 --> 00:08:45.085
but around the fourth meeting, you know,

00:08:45.085 --> 00:08:46.183
we used to always do the same thing.

00:08:46.183 --> 00:08:48.367
We would get there, he eats really late,

00:08:48.367 --> 00:08:51.178
so it would be, like, 8:30,
nine o'clock at night,

00:08:51.178 --> 00:08:54.974
and we would go, a few times,
'til just everybody had left.

00:08:54.974 --> 00:08:55.969
And it was kind of one of those things,

00:08:55.969 --> 00:08:57.889
there was, like, hardly anybody there,

00:08:57.889 --> 00:08:59.677
and he just, there was like a flip.

00:08:59.677 --> 00:09:00.693
A switch kind of flipped.

00:09:00.693 --> 00:09:04.860
He, I think he sort of
really committed to doing it,

00:09:07.129 --> 00:09:10.080
and he became much more
relaxed and easygoing.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.122
- And the number one book
for small business owners

00:09:12.122 --> 00:09:14.763
is The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People

00:09:14.763 --> 00:09:16.180
by Stephen Covey.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:20.658
- Stephen Covey is here.

00:09:20.658 --> 00:09:24.446
He is one of the big
bestsellers and one of the gurus

00:09:24.446 --> 00:09:26.742
in the world of personal leadership,

00:09:26.742 --> 00:09:28.459
taking control of your life.

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:30.084
He has spoke with President Clinton.

00:09:30.084 --> 00:09:32.563
His book The 7 Habits of
Highly Effective People

00:09:32.563 --> 00:09:34.152
is a big bestseller and has been

00:09:34.152 --> 00:09:36.347
on the bestseller list
for a very long time.

00:09:36.347 --> 00:09:38.140
Where did this book come from, I mean,

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:39.922
what makes the element of this book?

00:09:39.922 --> 00:09:43.326
- Well, essentially, about
20 years of just working

00:09:43.326 --> 00:09:45.215
with organizations and with people

00:09:45.215 --> 00:09:47.684
and trying to find what
are the common denominators

00:09:47.684 --> 00:09:50.017
that represent true success.

00:09:51.449 --> 00:09:54.744
Not just monetary success, but a balanced,

00:09:54.744 --> 00:09:57.274
long-term as well as short-term success,

00:09:57.274 --> 00:09:59.864
and that literature review study,

00:09:59.864 --> 00:10:02.901
going back to 1776, was very significant

00:10:02.901 --> 00:10:06.818
because I just learned
that the popular culture

00:10:09.606 --> 00:10:13.030
must focus upon principles
that do not change.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:15.661
Otherwise, the popular
culture is all based

00:10:15.661 --> 00:10:19.857
on a flawed foundation,
and for the first 150 years

00:10:19.857 --> 00:10:22.874
while we were on the
farm, essentially, it did.

00:10:22.874 --> 00:10:25.923
It focused upon fundamental principles

00:10:25.923 --> 00:10:29.406
like diligence, industry, integrity,

00:10:29.406 --> 00:10:32.323
honesty, openness, service, growth.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.633
But then, when we moved from the farm

00:10:35.633 --> 00:10:37.827
into the industrial age, it became

00:10:37.827 --> 00:10:39.950
more abstracted from reality, then we move

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:43.557
into the information age,
even more abstracted.

00:10:43.557 --> 00:10:47.140
'Til today, we're in
heavy into techniques,

00:10:48.728 --> 00:10:52.466
and technologies, and image building,

00:10:52.466 --> 00:10:54.894
and have pulled away to a great extent,

00:10:54.894 --> 00:10:57.850
in my judgment, from our character roots.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.999
- So your call to your audience

00:11:00.999 --> 00:11:03.214
is to come back to basic values?

00:11:03.214 --> 00:11:04.199
- Right.

00:11:04.199 --> 00:11:08.700
Come back, restore the
character ethic in our culture,

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:12.104
and then we can restore trust
inside our institutions.

00:11:12.104 --> 00:11:15.104
Otherwise, we're always, in a sense,

00:11:16.054 --> 00:11:19.620
playing on the surface and
living for appearances,

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:22.383
but in the long run, particularly
in today's global economy,

00:11:22.383 --> 00:11:25.421
you can't compete unless
you have high trust cultures

00:11:25.421 --> 00:11:27.818
to produce the kind of
quality that can compete.

00:11:27.818 --> 00:11:30.673
- The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People.

00:11:30.673 --> 00:11:33.314
Beginning with number
one, to be proactive.

00:11:33.314 --> 00:11:35.864
- Yeah, be proactive basically means

00:11:35.864 --> 00:11:38.495
take responsibility, take initiative.

00:11:38.495 --> 00:11:39.948
You are not an animal.

00:11:39.948 --> 00:11:41.289
- [Interviewer] Don't wait
for it to happen to you,

00:11:41.289 --> 00:11:42.477
seize the moment.

00:11:42.477 --> 00:11:45.644
- Seize the moment, don't be a victim.

00:11:46.856 --> 00:11:49.964
Don't get your mind into the
weaknesses of other people,

00:11:49.964 --> 00:11:53.855
like your spouse or your
boss or some institution,

00:11:53.855 --> 00:11:55.794
accept the fact that you have the power

00:11:55.794 --> 00:11:59.442
to choose your response in any
given set of circumstances.

00:11:59.442 --> 00:12:00.744
- Second is?

00:12:00.744 --> 00:12:02.880
- Second, begin with the end in mind.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:06.250
Get a clear sense of
what your life is about.

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.729
What your values are, and
what you're going to live by,

00:12:08.729 --> 00:12:12.003
and what your sense of mission is.

00:12:12.003 --> 00:12:14.915
- So if you're going to
play in the Super Bowl,

00:12:14.915 --> 00:12:18.298
then the Cowboys better have a mission,

00:12:18.298 --> 00:12:20.066
and they better know what they expect

00:12:20.066 --> 00:12:22.514
at the end of two halves.

00:12:22.514 --> 00:12:27.451
- Right, and also, they may
have a lot of short-term goals

00:12:27.451 --> 00:12:29.148
that would lead toward that mission,

00:12:29.148 --> 00:12:31.332
so that would be beginning
with the end in mind.

00:12:31.332 --> 00:12:33.354
It's the intellectual creation.

00:12:33.354 --> 00:12:36.025
Where habit three, putting
first things first,

00:12:36.025 --> 00:12:38.535
is the physical creation.

00:12:38.535 --> 00:12:39.987
In other words, you do it.

00:12:39.987 --> 00:12:43.136
Habit two says, "This is
the blueprint for my house,"

00:12:43.136 --> 00:12:46.712
habit three says, "Now construct it."

00:12:46.712 --> 00:12:48.379
Four, think win-win.

00:12:49.475 --> 00:12:50.725
Mutual benefit.

00:12:51.619 --> 00:12:53.773
See, not win-lose.

00:12:53.773 --> 00:12:56.485
Not lose-win, don't be a martyr.

00:12:56.485 --> 00:12:57.318
- [Interviewer] Win-win.

00:12:57.318 --> 00:12:58.740
- Yeah, win-win.
- I win, you win.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.863
- Right, in all situations.

00:13:00.863 --> 00:13:05.221
Even if I have to advocate
what is the win for you.

00:13:05.221 --> 00:13:06.634
- [Interviewer] Because if
you don't win, I don't win.

00:13:06.634 --> 00:13:09.696
- Number five is seek first to understand,

00:13:09.696 --> 00:13:10.951
then to be understood.

00:13:10.951 --> 00:13:11.784
- [Interviewer] Right, this is one you

00:13:11.784 --> 00:13:13.043
don't always get right, isn't it?

00:13:13.043 --> 00:13:14.626
- Oh, yeah.

00:13:14.626 --> 00:13:17.360
This is the basis of all
professionalism, really.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:20.422
To always understand before you judge.

00:13:20.422 --> 00:13:21.757
That's the one most people blow,

00:13:21.757 --> 00:13:23.803
that's the one I blow the most.

00:13:23.803 --> 00:13:25.465
Really.
- Understand before you...

00:13:25.465 --> 00:13:27.391
- Before you judge, before you act,

00:13:27.391 --> 00:13:30.034
before you seek to be understood.

00:13:30.034 --> 00:13:31.436
- Understand before
you're understood, even.

00:13:31.436 --> 00:13:35.083
- Diagnose someone's eyes
before you prescribe glasses.

00:13:35.083 --> 00:13:37.704
Listen to your child and the problem,

00:13:37.704 --> 00:13:40.060
and be sure you understand
before you rush in

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:42.163
with your quick solutions, see.

00:13:42.163 --> 00:13:44.530
- Part of that, also,
is before you even think

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:47.405
about a reply, just think
about understanding.

00:13:47.405 --> 00:13:49.772
- You listen with the
intent to understand,

00:13:49.772 --> 00:13:51.855
not with the intent to reply.

00:13:51.855 --> 00:13:56.050
You can see why that
necessitates internal security

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.387
that comes from the first three habits

00:13:58.387 --> 00:14:00.601
because otherwise, you're at risk,

00:14:00.601 --> 00:14:02.389
you don't know what's going to happen.

00:14:02.389 --> 00:14:05.925
You have to be very
genuinely open to listen

00:14:05.925 --> 00:14:08.129
within their frame of reference,

00:14:08.129 --> 00:14:10.567
rather than to kind of politely listen

00:14:10.567 --> 00:14:12.294
and then prepare your own response.

00:14:12.294 --> 00:14:14.290
- Yeah, exactly, I understand.

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:15.841
That's a difficult one, too.

00:14:15.841 --> 00:14:16.674
Number six.

00:14:16.674 --> 00:14:19.640
- Number six, synergize,
which basically means

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:23.429
create together something that is better

00:14:23.429 --> 00:14:24.932
than what I brought to the table

00:14:24.932 --> 00:14:27.035
or what you brought to the table.

00:14:27.035 --> 00:14:28.224
- [Interviewer] Number seven.

00:14:28.224 --> 00:14:31.391
- Sharpen the saw is the renewal habit

00:14:33.147 --> 00:14:36.255
of, you know, physically exercising,

00:14:36.255 --> 00:14:39.201
mentally sharpening the saw.

00:14:39.201 --> 00:14:42.036
Continue your education spiritually,

00:14:42.036 --> 00:14:45.093
recommit yourself to your
ultimate value system,

00:14:45.093 --> 00:14:47.907
and to live by your conscience, see,

00:14:47.907 --> 00:14:49.533
that's sharpening the saw.

00:14:49.533 --> 00:14:50.833
- So thank you guys so much for watching,

00:14:50.833 --> 00:14:51.798
I hope you enjoyed.

00:14:51.798 --> 00:14:54.521
I'd love to know, have you
read these books, any of them?

00:14:54.521 --> 00:14:57.213
Have they had an impact
on your business somehow?

00:14:57.213 --> 00:14:58.818
Are you planning on reading some of them

00:14:58.818 --> 00:15:00.616
after watching this video?

00:15:00.616 --> 00:15:02.160
Which ones and why?

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.324
Leave your thoughts down
in the comments below.

00:15:04.324 --> 00:15:05.868
I'm really curious to hear from you,

00:15:05.868 --> 00:15:08.601
and maybe this series will also continue.

00:15:08.601 --> 00:15:09.982
Thank you, guys, again, for watching.

00:15:09.982 --> 00:15:11.171
I believe in you.

00:15:11.171 --> 00:15:13.406
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself!

00:15:13.406 --> 00:15:15.488
And whatever your one word is.

00:15:15.488 --> 00:15:16.992
Much love.

00:15:16.992 --> 00:15:18.492
I'll see you soon.

